<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Headaches: Best Points" description="Learn how to use acupressure for headache relief. Step-by-step guide to LI4, GB20, and other effective points for tension headaches and migraines." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Headaches", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-headache/" }]} datePublished="2025-04-05" dateModified="2026-06-01" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Headache Relief: Best Points That Work</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Headaches are one of the most common health complaints worldwide. Whether tension headaches, migraines, or sinus headaches, the pain can disrupt work, family life, and basic daily function. Acupressure for headache relief offers a drug-free, self-administered approach rooted in traditional Chinese medicine and examined in modern clinical research.</p><div class="mb-8 p-4 bg-forest-50 dark:bg-forest-900/20 border-l-4 border-forest-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm"><strong>Research Note:</strong> A randomized controlled trial by Hsieh et al. (Am J Chin Med, 2010, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20128040/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300 dark:hover:text-forest-200">PMID 20128040</a>) found that one month of acupressure was more effective than a muscle relaxant for chronic headache. Clinical trials in this area often use the points GB20, LI4, and Taiyang. Reviews describe the overall evidence as promising but limited in quality, largely because blinding is difficult in acupressure research.</p></div><section id="types" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">What Are the Types of Headaches?</h2><p class="mb-3">Not all headaches are the same, and matching the right acupressure strategy to your headache type improves outcomes significantly.</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-3"><li><strong>Tension headaches</strong> — the most common type, presenting as a dull, band-like pressure around the forehead or back of the head. Often triggered by stress, poor posture, or eye strain.</li><li><strong>Migraines</strong> — moderate to severe throbbing pain, often on one side, accompanied by nausea, light sensitivity, and visual disturbances. Migraines involve neurological and vascular changes.</li><li><strong>Cervicogenic headaches</strong> — originating from the neck or cervical spine, typically felt on one side of the head and worsened by neck movement.</li><li><strong>Sinus headaches</strong> — deep, constant pain in the forehead, cheekbones, or bridge of the nose, accompanying sinus congestion.</li></ul></section><section id="how-acupressure-helps" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">How Does Acupressure Help Headaches?</h2><p class="mb-3">Acupressure may help headaches by stimulating pain-signaling pathways and relaxing tense muscles, though researchers are still working out the exact mechanism. Most trials in this area are small and hard to blind, since participants know when pressure is being applied. The leading hypotheses — that firm point stimulation influences pain-signaling pathways or promotes muscle relaxation — have not been firmly established, and honest reviews describe the evidence as promising but limited in quality.</p><p class="mb-3">In traditional Chinese medicine theory, most headaches are described as Qi and blood stagnation in the head region, often triggered by stress, poor diet, or external factors like wind and cold, and acupressure is said to restore smooth flow and relieve the stagnation that produces pain. This is traditional theory to explain why certain points are chosen, not a scientifically proven mechanism.</p></section><section id="best-points" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">What Are the 5 Best Acupressure Points for Headache Relief?</h2><p class="mb-4">The following five points cover the most common headache patterns. Use them individually for acute relief or in sequence for comprehensive coverage.</p><div class="mb-8 p-4 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-900/20 border-l-4 border-amber-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm"><strong>General Technique:</strong> Use your thumb pad to apply firm, steady pressure. Hold each point for 45-60 seconds with small circular motions. Breathe deeply throughout. You should feel a dull ache or warmth, never sharp pain. Repeat on both sides where applicable.</p></div><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3"><a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">LI4 (Hegu)</a> — Joining Valley</h3><p class="mb-2"><strong>Location:</strong> On the back of the hand, in the web between the thumb and index finger. See the <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:underline">full LI4 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-2"><strong>Why it works:</strong> LI4 is the most widely used point for headache in clinical practice. In TCM terms it is said to regulate Qi flow through the face and head, and it is particularly favored for frontal headaches, tension headaches, and sinus pressure.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Pinch the web between your thumb and index finger of the opposite hand. Press firmly toward the index finger bone. Hold for 60 seconds, maintaining steady pressure. You may feel a dull ache radiating along the index finger. Repeat on the opposite hand.</p><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3"><a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GB20 (Fengchi)</a> — Wind Pool</h3><p class="mb-2"><strong>Location:</strong> At the base of the skull, in the hollows between the two large neck muscles, about two inches apart. See the <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:underline">GB20 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-2"><strong>Why it works:</strong> GB20 is the standard point for headaches originating at the back of the head, including tension headaches and cervicogenic headaches. It is traditionally used to release neck tension that can refer pain upward into the head.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Place both thumbs on the depressions at the base of your skull, fingers resting on top of your head. Press upward into the hollows with firm pressure. Hold for 60 seconds while tilting your head back slightly against your thumbs. This point often releases significant tension.</p><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3"><a href="/points/taiyang-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">Taiyang (EX-HN5)</a> — Sun Point</h3><p class="mb-2"><strong>Location:</strong> In the temple region, in the depression about one finger-width behind the midpoint between the outer corner of the eye and the eyebrow.</p><p class="mb-2"><strong>Why it works:</strong> Taiyang is a local point that directly addresses temple pain and pressure. It is traditionally used for tension headaches, migraines localized to the side of the head, and eye strain headaches.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Use your middle fingers to press both temples simultaneously. Apply gentle circular pressure for 60 seconds. Many people find this point responds best to a lighter touch combined with slow, deep breathing.</p><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3"><a href="/points/gv20-baihui-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GV20 (Baihui)</a> — Hundred Meetings</h3><p class="mb-2"><strong>Location:</strong> On the very top of the head, along the midline, approximately at the midpoint between the ears when a line is drawn from ear to ear over the crown.</p><p class="mb-2"><strong>Why it works:</strong> In TCM, GV20 is described as a meeting point of all Yang channels, traditionally used to clear the head and sharpens mental clarity. It is favored for headaches accompanied by dizziness, brain fog, or a heavy-headed sensation.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Use your index finger or knuckle to press firmly on the crown of your head. Hold for 45-60 seconds. This point can be stimulated with small circular motions or steady downward pressure.</p><h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-3"><a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">PC6 (Neiguan)</a> — Inner Gate</h3><p class="mb-2"><strong>Location:</strong> Three finger-widths below the wrist crease on the inner forearm, between the two tendons. See the <a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:underline">PC6 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-2"><strong>Why it works:</strong> PC6 is especially valuable for migraines accompanied by nausea. It is traditionally used to relieve both the headache pain and the gastrointestinal symptoms that often accompany migraine episodes.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Turn your arm palm-up. Place your thumb between the two tendons and press firmly. Hold for 60 seconds, breathing deeply. Repeat on the other arm.</p></section><section id="routine" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">What Is the Step-by-Step Headache Relief Routine?</h2><p class="mb-3">When a headache strikes, follow this sequence for relief:</p><ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-3"><li><strong>GB20 (90 seconds each side):</strong> Start at the base of the skull to release neck tension that drives many headaches.</li><li><strong>Taiyang (60 seconds each side):</strong> Move to the temples for local relief. Use gentle circular pressure.</li><li><strong>LI4 (60 seconds each hand):</strong> Apply firm pressure to LI4. This point is widely used for general headache relief and can be repeated if needed.</li><li><strong>GV20 (60 seconds):</strong> Stimulate the crown of the head to ease lingering fogginess and pressure.</li><li><strong>PC6 (60 seconds each arm):</strong> Finish with PC6, especially if you feel nauseous.</li></ol><p>After the routine, rest in a dark, quiet room for 10-15 minutes. Avoid screens and bright light during recovery.</p></section><section id="prevention" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">How Can I Prevent Headaches?</h2><p class="mb-3">Daily acupressure may help some people reduce headache frequency over time. Here are additional prevention strategies:</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-3"><li>Practice 3-5 minutes of acupressure each morning, focusing on GB20 and LI4, to address tension before it builds.</li><li>Maintain good posture, especially during desk work. Forward head posture is a leading cause of cervicogenic headaches.</li><li>Stay hydrated. Dehydration is a common trigger for both tension headaches and migraines.</li><li>Identify and avoid personal trigger foods, such as aged cheeses, processed meats, or artificial sweeteners.</li><li>Consider keeping a headache diary to identify patterns — note timing, intensity, triggers, and which acupressure points seemed to help.</li></ul></section><section id="evidence" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">What Does the Evidence Say?</h2><h3 class="mb-3 font-semibold text-lg">What Do the Key Studies Find?</h3><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-3"><li><strong>Chronic headache (2010 RCT):</strong> Hsieh et al. (Am J Chin Med, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20128040/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300 dark:hover:text-forest-200">PMID 20128040</a>) found one month of acupressure more effective than a muscle relaxant for chronic headache — one of the better-designed studies in this area.</li><li><strong>Points used in trials:</strong> Studies commonly stimulate GB20 (base of skull), LI4 (hand web), and Taiyang (temples) for headache.</li><li><strong>Review status:</strong> Reviews of acupressure for pain and symptoms (Lee EJ &amp; Frazier SK, 2011, <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21531533/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300 dark:hover:text-forest-200">PMID 21531533</a>; Chen YW &amp; Wang HH, 2014, PMID 23415783) describe the evidence for headache as promising but limited in quality, largely because blinding is difficult.</li><li><strong>What reviews do not show:</strong> No reliable pooled effect size or percentage reduction is yet well established, so be skeptical of any specific "X% reduction" figure.</li></ul></section><section id="faq" class="mb-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="mb-6"><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">Can acupressure cure migraines?</h3><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Acupressure is not a cure for migraines, but it is a reasonable tool to try alongside conventional migraine care for relieving discomfort and possibly reducing frequency. Many people use it to improve overall headache management.</p></div><div class="mb-6"><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">How quickly does acupressure work for headaches?</h3><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Some people notice relief within 15-30 minutes of applying steady pressure; LI4 and GB20 are often the fastest points to respond. If there is no improvement after about 30 minutes, consider other treatment options.</p></div><div class="mb-6"><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">Can I do acupressure during a migraine attack?</h3><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Yes, but use lighter pressure than usual, as sensitivity is often heightened during migraines. PC6 is especially helpful for migraine-associated nausea. Avoid bright lights and stimulate points in a dark, quiet room.</p></div><div class="mb-6"><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">Is it safe to use LI4 during pregnancy?</h3><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">LI4 is generally avoided during pregnancy because it is traditionally thought to stimulate uterine contractions. Pregnant individuals are usually advised to focus on GB20, Taiyang, and GV20 instead. Always consult your healthcare provider before using acupressure during pregnancy.</p></div><div class="mb-6"><h3 class="font-semibold mb-1">When should I see a doctor for headaches?</h3><p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Seek medical attention if you experience sudden, severe headaches (thunderclap headaches), headaches accompanied by fever, neck stiffness, vision changes, slurred speech, weakness, or if your headache pattern changes significantly. Also consult a doctor if headaches occur more than 15 days per month.</p></div></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This content is for informational purposes only and is not medical advice. Consult a licensed healthcare provider before starting any new wellness practice.</p></div>
